{
"Before practice, mountains are mountains. Then they are not mountains. At the end, they are just mountains again.": "539e1b5f8215",
"Chan, which became Zen in Japan, takes its name from dhyāna, meditation, and it describes itself as a direct pointing at the mind. Its most famous text is the Platform Sūtra of the sixth patriarch Huineng. When a monk wrote that the mind is like a mirror that must be wiped clean, Huineng, an illiterate kitchen worker, had someone write his reply: Bodhi originally has no tree; the bright mirror is no stand. Originally there is not a single thing. Where could dust alight? The sūtras teaching is precise. From the beginning this Dharma gate has taken no-thought as its doctrine, no-form as its substance, and non-abiding as its root. And no-thought is carefully defined: No-thought means, in thought, not being caught by thought. Huineng warns against the obvious misreading: If you simply think of nothing, and get rid of all thought, then when thought is cut off you die and are reborn elsewhere. That is a great mistake.": "c64d73997c8b",
"Heidegger argued that modern Western thought has become calculative. It treats everything, nature and people included, as a resource to be measured, ordered, and used. Against this he described another kind of thinking, which he called meditative, and an attitude he named Gelassenheit, often translated as releasement or letting-be: a readiness to let things show themselves as they are, without seizing them for our purposes. His late writings return again and again to the plain things of a rural life, a jug, a bridge, a path through the fields. Japanese philosophers of the Kyoto School studied with him, and there are well-known reports that he was struck by D. T. Suzukis books on Zen. The parallel with Chan is real. Both distrust the grasping mind, both look for truth in ordinary things seen without grasping, and both are suspicious of systems. The difference is that Heideggers letting-be has no practice attached to it. He offers no method of sitting, no teacher, no precepts. Chan without practice would be, for the masters, only talk about the moon.": "853cd6f8314f",
"Huineng said that trying to think of nothing is a great mistake. No-thought means not being caught by thought.": "ec02de67ba88",
"In his later work, the Philosophical Investigations, Wittgenstein came to see philosophical problems as confusions produced when language goes on holiday, when words are taken out of the ordinary activities that give them sense. The cure is not a new theory but a patient return to how words are actually used, until the problem disappears. He said that philosophy should leave everything as it is, and that its aim is to show the fly the way out of the fly-bottle. This is very close to the third stage of Qingyuans sermon. After the confusion, mountains are just mountains again, the same as before and yet seen differently. Wittgenstein also taught in a way that resembles a Chan teachers: by examples, questions, and reminders, without doctrines to memorize. The difference is the scope of the confusion. For Wittgenstein the bottle is made of philosophical puzzles. For the Chan masters it is made of grasping and the sense of a separate self, and the way out is not only clearer thinking but a change in how one lives. The lesson on the four corners and the lesson on emptiness looked at the earlier and later Wittgenstein from other angles.": "7dbc0898687d",
"Linji said the Buddhadharma is just wearing clothes, eating rice, and lying down when tired.": "41324c45bb3d",
"Most Westerners already have an idea of Zen, often from design, tea, or self-help books, and it usually involves an empty mind and a clever paradox. Start by correcting the first gently with Huinengs own words: thinking of nothing is a great mistake, and no-thought means not being caught by thought. Then tell Qingyuans sermon about the mountains, which almost everyone finds memorable, and ask which of the three stages the audience is in. For philosophy students, introduce Nishida as the person who brought Zen into Western philosophy, with William James pure experience as the link. Make the two points the West tends to miss: Zen is a discipline with teachers and precepts, and it ends in compassion. Close with the ox-herding picture of the sage entering the marketplace.": "50c44f88f38a",
"Nishida Kitarō trained in Zen and borrowed William James term pure experience to describe it.": "a2985b9ffadc",
"Nishida Kitarō, the founder of the Kyoto School, practiced Zen intensively for about ten years while teaching philosophy, and his first book, An Inquiry into the Good, opens with pure experience. He took the term from William James, who had written that the instant field of the present is at all times what I call the pure experience. It is only virtually or potentially either object or subject as yet. For James it is plain, unqualified actuality, or existence, a simple that. Nishida made this the foundation of everything: before there is a knower and a known, there is experience itself, and the self and the world are later abstractions from it. A musician absorbed in playing, he suggested, does not experience a subject and an object. Readers who know Huinengs no-thought hear the echo at once. Nishida went on to develop a philosophy of place and of absolute nothingness, using Western terms to say what Zen had shown him. He is the closest parallel because he is not a parallel at all. He is the point where the two traditions met in one person.": "d0ecfc773c0d",
"The best summary of the path is a short sermon by the Song dynasty master Qingyuan Weixin, recorded in the Compendium of the Five Lamps. Thirty years ago, before I studied Chan, I saw mountains as mountains and rivers as rivers. Later, when I met a teacher and gained an entry, I saw that mountains are not mountains and rivers are not rivers. Now that I have found a place of rest, I see mountains as just mountains and rivers as just rivers. Everyone, are these three views the same or different? The second stage is emptiness, the discovery that nothing has the fixed nature it seemed to have. The third is the return, where the ordinary world is seen without grasping. Chan did not reject the rest of Buddhism. The Platform Sūtra is full of the precepts, the bodhisattva vows, and the perfection of wisdom, and the Chinese masters joined Chan with Pure Land practice too. Yunqi Zhuhong, asked whether Chan is faster than reciting the Buddhas name, compared them to two people traveling to the same treasure, one on horseback and one by boat: When traveling far, arriving is what matters.": "5eed3a3cdf9c",
"The closest is Nishida, because he practiced Zen and wrote philosophy from it, and his pure experience, borrowed from James, names what Huinengs no-thought describes: experience before it splits into a self and its objects. Heidegger is closest on the attitude, letting things be instead of seizing them, and on the turn to plain things. Wittgenstein is closest on the shape of the path, a confusion that ends by returning to the ordinary, where mountains are just mountains.": "6cd2181f5d52",
"The first further step is that Chan insists on practice, and the West has often missed this. Since the 1950s Zen has been presented in Western books as spontaneity, paradox, and freedom from rules, and the image of the eccentric master who burns buddha statues has traveled better than the image of the monk who sits for decades. Linjis line that the Buddhadharma is just eating and sleeping was spoken to monks who had already spent years in strict training. Huineng was explicit that suppressing thought is a mistake, and he was just as explicit that the teaching is for practice: Self-awakening and practice do not lie in argument. For a Western teacher, the most useful thing to say about Zen may be that it is a discipline. The spontaneity comes at the end of the training. It does not replace the training.": "3eaf787721c9",
"The last ox-herding picture shows the sage returning to the marketplace to help others.": "53a427792e83",
"The likeness ends at practice and compassion. Each Western thinker reached his insight by thinking and wrote it down as philosophy. The Chan masters said that seeing ones nature comes through practice, sitting, kōans, precepts, and a teacher, and that it shows in how one wears clothes and eats rice. Nishida himself kept these two apart: his books are philosophy, and his Zen was done in the meditation hall. And Chan belongs to the Mahāyāna. In the Platform Sūtra Huineng has his listeners take the four great vows, the first of which is to save all beings. The return to the ordinary in the third stage is also a return to other people. Huinengs mountains are just mountains, and they are also the place where a bodhisattva works.": "5f3d820e3c6c",
"The same sūtra refuses to separate meditation from wisdom. Concentration and wisdom are one body, not two. Concentration is the body of wisdom, and wisdom is the function of concentration, like a lamp and its light. Later masters brought this down to daily life. Linji, in the ninth century, said that the Buddhadharma has no place for special effort: it is just being ordinary, with nothing to do: relieving oneself, wearing clothes, eating rice, lying down when tired. And then: Be master wherever you are, and wherever you stand is true. Chan teachers also used the public case, the kōan, to break the habit of conceptual grasping. A monk asked Zhaozhou whether a dog has buddha-nature, and Zhaozhou said, Wu, no. The Gateless Barrier makes that single word its first case: To study Chan you must pass the barrier of the patriarchs. Wondrous awakening requires that the road of the mind be cut off.": "d25cac0dcbe5",
"The second step is that the return to the ordinary is a return to other people. The old Chinese series of ox-herding pictures, which shows the search for the self as a search for a lost ox, does not end with an empty circle. Its last picture shows the sage entering the marketplace with open hands, among ordinary people. Western philosophers who approached Zen mostly stopped at the empty circle, the insight, or the letting-be. The Chan masters went on. The Platform Sūtra includes the vow to save all beings, the masters spent their lives teaching, and Yunqi Zhuhong taught monks and laypeople alike that Chan and the Pure Land arrive at the same treasure. This is where the whole shelf ends. Each lesson has compared a Buddhist idea with its Western neighbors, and each time the likeness ended at the purpose. For Buddhism the purpose of every idea is the end of suffering, ones own and that of others.": "eaf2e7b05d3a",
"Zen is a discipline. The spontaneity comes at the end of the training. It does not replace it.": "9af96ad5914b"
}
